<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-extreme-values" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Extreme Values and Saddle Points</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Understand what it means for a function of two variables to have a local
maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point, and visualize each on the
surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Recognize critical points as the candidates for local extrema, and understand
why a vanishing gradient is necessary but not sufficient for an extremum.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Understand the idea behind the Second Derivative Test and what it reveals
about the local shape of a surface near a critical point.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Distinguish local extrema from absolute extrema, and understand why the
absolute extrema of a function on a closed, bounded region may occur on the
boundary.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Interpret extreme values in a physical setting, such as the equilibrium and
stability of a system.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-local-extrema">
<title>Local Maximum and Local Minimum</title>

<definition xml:id="def-local-extrema">
<title>Local Maximum and Local Minimum</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let the domain of the function <m>f(x,y)</m> contain the point
<m>(a,b)</m>. Then
</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>f(a,b)</m> is a <term>local minimum</term> if
<m>f(a,b) \le f(x,y)</m> for all the points <m>(x,y)</m> in an open
disk centered at <m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>f(a,b)</m> is a <term>local maximum</term> if
<m>f(a,b) \ge f(x,y)</m> for all the points <m>(x,y)</m> in an open
disk centered at <m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
As the first example, consider the function <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2</m>.
We have already studied this function and we know that it represents a
paraboloid. Note that we can find a disk centered at the point
<m>(0,0)</m> such that <m>f(0,0) \le f(x,y)</m> for all the points
<m>(x,y)</m> inside the disk, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-local-min-disk"/>, and hence by definition
<m>f(0,0)</m> is a local minimum of <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2</m>, as shown
in <xref ref="fig-local-min-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-local-min-video">
<caption>
The paraboloid <m>z = x^2 + y^2</m>. Over an open disk centered at
<m>(0,0)</m>, the value <m>f(0,0) = 0</m> is smaller than every other
value of the function, so <m>f(0,0)</m> is a local minimum.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-local-min"
       source="videos/local_min_paraboloid_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/local_min_paraboloid_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-local-min-disk">
<caption>
An open disk in the domain of <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2</m> centered at
<m>(0,0)</m>. For every point <m>(x,y)</m> inside the disk we have
<m>f(0,0) \le f(x,y)</m>, so <m>f(0,0)</m> is a local minimum.
</caption>
<image width="50%">
<shortdescription>An open disk centred at the origin, where the function has a local minimum.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
An open disk in the x y plane, centered at the origin, drawn with a dashed red boundary and shaded gray. The center point 0, 0 is marked in green. Inside the disk, a label reads f of 0, 0 is less than or equal to f of x, y.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-ev-local-min-disk"><![CDATA[
// An open disk centred at (0,0) in the domain of f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2: inside it
// f(0,0) <= f(x,y), so f(0,0) is a local minimum.
import graph;
unitsize(2.4cm);

// axes through the origin, with arrowheads and no tick marks
void axes(real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax, string xl, string yl) {
  pen axispen = black + linewidth(0.8pt);
  draw((xmin, 0)--(xmax, 0), axispen, Arrow(6));
  draw((0, ymin)--(0, ymax), axispen, Arrow(6));
  if (xl != "") label(xl, (xmax, 0), E);
  if (yl != "") label(yl, (0, ymax), N);
}

// the open disk: shaded, with a dashed boundary because the boundary is left out
path disk = circle((0, 0), 1.1);
fill(disk, gray(0.85));
draw(disk, red + linewidth(1.4pt) + linetype("6 4"));

axes(-1.55, 1.55, -1.55, 1.55, "$x$", "$y$");

dot((0, 0), rgb(0, 0.5, 0) + linewidth(5pt));
label("$(0,0)$", (0, 0), NE, rgb(0, 0.5, 0));
label("$f(0,0)\le f(x,y)$", (0, -0.55), Fill(gray(0.85)));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The next example is the function <m>f(x,y) = -x^2 - y^2</m>. Note that
we can find a disk centered at the point <m>(0,0)</m> such that
<m>f(0,0) \ge f(x,y)</m> for all the points <m>(x,y)</m> inside the
disk and hence by definition <m>f(0,0)</m> is a local maximum of
<m>f(x,y) = -x^2 - y^2</m>, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-local-max-video"/> and <xref ref="fig-local-max-trace"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-local-max-video">
<caption>
The paraboloid <m>z = -x^2 - y^2</m> opens downward. Over an open disk
centered at <m>(0,0)</m>, the value <m>f(0,0) = 0</m> is larger than
every other value of the function, so <m>f(0,0)</m> is a local maximum.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-local-max"
       source="videos/local_max_paraboloid_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/local_max_paraboloid_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-local-max-trace">
<caption>
The trace of the surface <m>z = -x^2 - y^2</m> in the plane
<m>y = 0</m> is the parabola <m>z = -x^2</m>, which has a maximum at
the origin. By symmetry, every vertical cross-section through the
origin has the same shape.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>A parabola opening downward with its highest point at the origin.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
The parabola z equals negative x squared, opening downward, which is the trace of the surface z equals negative x squared minus y squared in the plane y equals 0. The highest point of the parabola is at the origin, marked in green as the local maximum.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-ev-local-max-trace"><![CDATA[
// The trace of z = -x^2 - y^2 in the plane y = 0: the parabola z = -x^2, with its
// maximum at the origin.
import graph;
unitsize(2.6cm);

// axes through the origin, with arrowheads and no tick marks
void axes(real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax, string xl, string yl) {
  pen axispen = black + linewidth(0.8pt);
  draw((xmin, 0)--(xmax, 0), axispen, Arrow(6));
  draw((0, ymin)--(0, ymax), axispen, Arrow(6));
  if (xl != "") label(xl, (xmax, 0), E);
  if (yl != "") label(yl, (0, ymax), N);
}

axes(-1.5, 1.5, -1.85, 0.6, "$x$", "$z$");

real g(real x) { return -x^2; }
draw(graph(g, -1.3, 1.3), blue + linewidth(1.6pt));
label("$z=-x^2$", (0.95, g(0.95)), E, blue);

dot((0, 0), rgb(0, 0.5, 0) + linewidth(5pt));
label("Local maximum", (0.08, 0.08), NE, rgb(0, 0.5, 0));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<theorem xml:id="thm-first-derivative-test">
<title>Theorem I</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>f(x,y)</m> has a local maximum or minimum at an interior point
<m>(a,b)</m> of its domain and if the first partial derivatives exist
there, then
<md xml:id="eq-first-derivative-test" number="yes">
f_x(a,b) = f_y(a,b) = 0.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
As an example, consider the function <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2</m>, which
has a local minimum at <m>(0,0)</m>. You can easily check that
<m>f_x(0,0) = f_y(0,0) = 0</m>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-critical-point">
<title>Critical Point</title>
<statement>
<p>
An interior point of the domain of a function <m>f(x,y)</m> where both
<m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m> are zero or where one or both of <m>f_x</m>
and <m>f_y</m> do not exist is a <term>critical point</term> of
<m>f</m>. Note that not every critical point is a local extremum.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<example xml:id="example-critical-point-zero-partials">
<title>A Critical Point Where Both Partial Derivatives Are Zero</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the critical points of the function
<m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 2x - 2y + 3</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<paragraphs xml:id="solution-critical-point-zero-partials">
<title>Solution</title>

<p>
The partial derivatives <m>f_x = 2x - 2</m> and <m>f_y = 2y - 2</m> exist
everywhere, so the critical points are the points where both of them are
zero. From <m>2x - 2 = 0</m> and <m>2y - 2 = 0</m> we get <m>x = 1</m> and
<m>y = 1</m>, so the only critical point is <m>(1,1)</m>, where
<m>f(1,1) = 1</m>.
</p>
<p>
The graph of <m>f</m> is the paraboloid in
<xref ref="fig-critical-point-zero-partials"/>. Completing the squares
gives <m>f(x,y) = (x-1)^2 + (y-1)^2 + 1 \ge 1</m>, so <m>f(1,1) = 1</m>
is a local minimum: the critical point sits under the lowest point of the
surface, where the tangent plane is horizontal.
</p>

</paragraphs>

<figure xml:id="fig-critical-point-zero-partials">
<caption>
The graph of <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 2x - 2y + 3</m>. At the critical
point <m>(1,1)</m> both partial derivatives are zero, so the tangent
plane to the surface at <m>(1,1,1)</m> is the horizontal plane
<m>z = 1</m>.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<shortdescription>A bowl-shaped surface resting on a horizontal plane that touches it at its lowest point.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A gray paraboloid opening upward, the graph of f. Its lowest point, at 1,
1, 1, is marked in magenta, directly above the point 1, 1 of the x y
plane, which is marked in dark red and joined to it by a dashed vertical
line. A pink horizontal plane, labelled tangent plane z equals 1, touches
the surface at the lowest point.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-ev-critical-point-zero-partials"><![CDATA[
// The paraboloid z = x^2 + y^2 - 2x - 2y + 3 = (x-1)^2 + (y-1)^2 + 1 with its critical
// point (1,1): both partial derivatives are zero there and the tangent plane is horizontal.
import graph3;
// Keep HTML interactive; use vector rendering only for print output.
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;
size(11cm,9cm);
currentprojection=orthographic(camera=(7,-5,4),up=Z);
currentlight=light(gray(0.6),specularfactor=0.2,(4,-3,6),(-4,-1,3));
pen ax=gray(0.25)+linewidth(0.6pt);

// the surface over the disc of radius 1.5 centred at (1,1)
triple bowl(pair q) { return (1 + q.x*cos(q.y), 1 + q.x*sin(q.y), 1 + q.x^2); }
draw(surface(bowl, (0, 0), (1.5, 2pi), 8, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=gray(0.75)+opacity(0.35),
     meshpen=gray(0.55)+opacity(0.4)+linewidth(0.3pt));

// the horizontal tangent plane z = 1 at the lowest point
triple A = (1, 1, 1);
path3 plane = (0.1, 0.1, 1)--(1.9, 0.1, 1)--(1.9, 1.9, 1)--(0.1, 1.9, 1)--cycle;
draw(surface(plane), rgb("e88a8a")+opacity(0.45), nolight);
draw(plane, rgb("993333")+linewidth(0.5pt));

// the critical point in the xy-plane and the point of the surface above it
pen guide = gray(0.45)+linewidth(0.6pt)+linetype("4 3");
draw((1, 0, 0)--(1, 1, 0)--(0, 1, 0), guide);
draw((1, 1, 0)--A, guide);
dot((1, 1, 0), rgb("7f0000")+linewidth(5pt));
dot(A, magenta+linewidth(5pt));
label("$(1,1)$", (1, 1, 0), 2*S, rgb("7f0000"));
label("$(1,1,1)$", A, 2*N+E, magenta);
label("tangent plane $z=1$", (0.1, 0.1, 1), 2*W, rgb("993333"));
label("$z=f(x,y)$", bowl((1.5, 0.3)), E, gray(0.4));
label("$1$", (1, 0, 0), S, fontsize(9pt));
label("$1$", (0, 1, 0), W, fontsize(9pt));

draw(Label("$x$",1),O--(3.2,0,0),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$y$",1),O--(0,3.2,0),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$z$",1),O--(0,0,3.7),ax,Arrow3);
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-critical-point-cone">
<title>A Critical Point Where the Partial Derivatives Do Not Exist</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the critical points of the function
<m>f(x,y) = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<paragraphs xml:id="solution-critical-point-cone">
<title>Solution</title>

<p>
Away from the origin the partial derivatives are
<md>
<mrow>f_x \amp= \frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}},</mrow>
<mrow>f_y \amp= \frac{y}{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}}.</mrow>
</md>
They are both zero only if <m>x = 0</m> and <m>y = 0</m>, which is the
one point where these formulas do not apply. So there is no critical
point of the first kind.
</p>
<p>
At the origin we go back to the definition of the partial derivative.
Since <m>f(h,0) = \sqrt{h^2} = |h|</m>,
<md>
\frac{f(h,0) - f(0,0)}{h} = \frac{|h|}{h},
</md>
which is <m>1</m> for <m>h \gt 0</m> and <m>-1</m> for <m>h \lt 0</m>.
The limit as <m>h \to 0</m> does not exist, so <m>f_x(0,0)</m> does not
exist, and in the same way neither does <m>f_y(0,0)</m>. Hence
<m>(0,0)</m> is a critical point of <m>f</m>, and it is the only one.
</p>
<p>
The graph of <m>f</m> is the cone in
<xref ref="fig-critical-point-cone"/>, and the critical point is its
sharp tip. Since <m>f(0,0) = 0 \le f(x,y)</m> for every <m>(x,y)</m>,
this critical point is also a local minimum.
</p>

</paragraphs>

<figure xml:id="fig-critical-point-cone">
<caption>
The graph of <m>f(x,y) = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2}</m> is a cone. Its trace in
the plane <m>y = 0</m> is <m>z = |x|</m> (green), which has a corner at
the origin: the partial derivatives do not exist there, so
<m>(0,0)</m> is a critical point.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<shortdescription>A cone standing on its tip at the origin, with a V-shaped curve drawn on it.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A gray cone opening upward with its tip at the origin, marked in magenta.
A green V-shaped curve, labelled z equals the absolute value of x, runs
down one side of the cone to the tip and up the opposite side: the trace
of the cone in the plane y equals 0, with a corner at the origin.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-ev-critical-point-cone"><![CDATA[
// The cone z = sqrt(x^2 + y^2): at (0,0) the surface has a sharp point, the partial
// derivatives do not exist, and the trace in the plane y = 0 is the V-shaped z = |x|.
import graph3;
// Keep HTML interactive; use vector rendering only for print output.
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;
size(11cm,9cm);
currentprojection=orthographic(camera=(7,-5,4),up=Z);
currentlight=light(gray(0.6),specularfactor=0.2,(4,-3,6),(-4,-1,3));
pen ax=gray(0.25)+linewidth(0.6pt);

// the cone over the disc of radius 1.6
triple cone(pair q) { return (q.x*cos(q.y), q.x*sin(q.y), q.x); }
draw(surface(cone, (0, 0), (1.6, 2pi), 8, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=gray(0.75)+opacity(0.35),
     meshpen=gray(0.55)+opacity(0.4)+linewidth(0.3pt));

// the trace in the plane y = 0: z = |x|, with a corner at the origin
pen trace = rgb("008000")+linewidth(1.8pt);
draw((-1.6, 0, 1.6)--O--(1.6, 0, 1.6), trace);
label("$z=|x|$", (1.6, 0, 1.6), 2*W+N, rgb("008000"));

dot(O, magenta+linewidth(6pt));
label("$(0,0,0)$", O, 2*S+E, magenta);
label("$z=f(x,y)$", cone((1.6, 1.2)), E, gray(0.4));

draw(Label("$x$",1),(-2,0,0)--(2.4,0,0),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$y$",1),(0,-2,0)--(0,2.4,0),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$z$",1),O--(0,0,2.3),ax,Arrow3);
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
In both examples the critical point turned out to be a local minimum.
The next subsection shows a critical point that is neither a local
maximum nor a local minimum.
</p>


</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-saddle-example">
<title>Saddle Point</title>

<definition xml:id="def-saddle-point">
<title>Saddle Point</title>
<statement>
<p>
A differentiable function <m>f(x,y)</m> has a <term>saddle point</term>
at a critical point <m>(a,b)</m> if in every open disk centered at
<m>(a,b)</m> there are domain points <m>(x,y)</m> where
<m>f(a,b) \lt f(x,y)</m> and domain points <m>(x,y)</m> where
<m>f(a,b) \gt f(x,y)</m>. The corresponding point
<m>(a,b,f(a,b))</m> on the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> is called a
saddle point of the surface.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<example xml:id="example-saddle-point">
<title>Saddle Point</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function
<md xml:id="eq-saddle-function" number="yes">
f(x,y) = \frac{y^2}{3} - \frac{x^2}{3}.
</md>
</p>
<p>
Show that <m>f</m> has a saddle point at <m>(0,0)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<paragraphs xml:id="solution-saddle-point">
<title>Solution</title>

<p>
As we previously studied, this function represents a hyperbolic
paraboloid, which is shown in <xref ref="fig-saddle-video"/>. Note that
the first partial derivatives are
<md xml:id="eq-saddle-partials" number="yes">
f_x = -\frac{2}{3}x \quad \text{and} \quad f_y = \frac{2}{3}y.
</md>
</p>
<p>
The partial derivatives exist everywhere, so the only point at which a
local extremum can occur is the point <m>(0,0)</m>. Note that along the
<m>x</m>-axis the function takes negative values,
<m>f(x,0) = -x^2/3 \lt 0</m>, whereas along the <m>y</m>-axis the
function takes positive values, <m>f(0,y) = y^2/3 \gt 0</m>, as shown
in <xref ref="fig-saddle-traces"/>. Hence inside every open disk around
<m>(0,0)</m>, there are points <m>(x,y)</m> such that
<m>f(x,y) \gt f(0,0)</m> and also there are points such that
<m>f(x,y) \lt f(0,0)</m>, which means <m>(0,0)</m> is a saddle point.
Note that in this example the two partial derivatives are zero at
<m>(0,0)</m>; however, the function does not have a local extremum at
this point.
</p>

</paragraphs>

<figure xml:id="fig-saddle-video">
<caption>
The hyperbolic paraboloid <m>z = y^2/3 - x^2/3</m>. Along the
<m>x</m>-axis the surface falls below the origin, and along the
<m>y</m>-axis it rises above the origin, so <m>(0,0)</m> is a saddle
point.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-saddle-point"
       source="videos/saddle_point_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/saddle_point_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-saddle-traces">
<caption>
The traces of <m>z = y^2/3 - x^2/3</m> along the two coordinate planes.
In the plane <m>y = 0</m> the trace <m>z = -x^2/3</m> opens downward,
while in the plane <m>x = 0</m> the trace <m>z = y^2/3</m> opens
upward, so <m>(0,0)</m> is a saddle point.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>Two parabolas through the origin, one opening upward and one opening downward.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
Two parabolas through the origin showing the traces of the hyperbolic paraboloid z equals y squared over 3 minus x squared over 3, drawn on one pair of axes: the horizontal axis stands for x or y and the vertical axis is z. The blue parabola opens upward and is the trace along the y axis. The red parabola opens downward and is the trace along the x axis. The origin, where they meet, is the saddle point.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-ev-saddle-traces"><![CDATA[
// The traces of z = y^2/3 - x^2/3 in the two coordinate planes, drawn on one pair of
// axes: z = y^2/3 (plane x = 0) opens upward, z = -x^2/3 (plane y = 0) opens downward.
import graph;
unitsize(1.7cm);

// axes through the origin, with arrowheads and no tick marks
void axes(real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax, string xl, string yl) {
  pen axispen = black + linewidth(0.8pt);
  draw((xmin, 0)--(xmax, 0), axispen, Arrow(6));
  draw((0, ymin)--(0, ymax), axispen, Arrow(6));
  if (xl != "") label(xl, (xmax, 0), E);
  if (yl != "") label(yl, (0, ymax), N);
}

axes(-2.4, 2.4, -1.7, 1.7, "$x$ or $y$", "$z$");

real up(real t) { return t^2/3; }
real down(real t) { return -t^2/3; }
draw(graph(up, -2.1, 2.1), blue + linewidth(1.6pt));
draw(graph(down, -2.1, 2.1), red + linewidth(1.6pt));
label("$\displaystyle z=\frac{y^2}{3}$", (1.45, up(1.45)), NW, blue);
label("$\displaystyle z=-\frac{x^2}{3}$", (1.45, down(1.45)), SW, red);

dot((0, 0), black + linewidth(5pt));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-second-derivative-test">
<title>The Second Derivative Test</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-second-derivative-test">
<title>Theorem II (The Second Derivative Test)</title>
<statement>
<p>
Suppose that <m>f(x,y)</m> and its first and second partial derivatives
are continuous throughout a disk centered at <m>(a,b)</m> and that
<m>f_x(a,b) = f_y(a,b) = 0</m>. Then
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="i.">
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> has a <term>local maximum</term> at <m>(a,b)</m> if
<m>f_{xx} \lt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 \gt 0</m> at
<m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> has a <term>local minimum</term> at <m>(a,b)</m> if
<m>f_{xx} \gt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 \gt 0</m> at
<m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> has a <term>saddle point</term> at <m>(a,b)</m> if
<m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 \lt 0</m> at <m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<em>The test is inconclusive</em> at <m>(a,b)</m> if
<m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = 0</m> at <m>(a,b)</m>. In this case, we
must find some other way to determine the behavior of <m>f</m> at
<m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
Note that the expression <m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2</m> is called the
<term>discriminant</term> or the <term>Hessian</term> of the function
<m>f(x,y)</m> and can be easily remembered when written in the
following form:
<md xml:id="eq-hessian" number="yes">
H(x,y) =
\begin{vmatrix}
f_{xx} \amp f_{xy} \\
f_{xy} \amp f_{yy}
\end{vmatrix}
= f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2.
</md>
</p>
<example xml:id="example-quartic-extrema">
<title>Finding Local Extrema and Saddle Points</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the local maximum and minimum values and saddle points of the
function
<md xml:id="eq-quartic-function" number="yes">
z = f(x,y) = x^4 + y^4 - 4xy.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<paragraphs xml:id="solution-quartic-extrema">
<title>Solution</title>

<p>
We begin by computing the partial derivatives:
<md xml:id="eq-quartic-partials" number="yes">
f_x = 4x^3 - 4y \quad \text{and} \quad f_y = 4y^3 - 4x.
</md>
</p>
<p>
We set both partials equal to zero, which gives us:
<md xml:id="eq-quartic-critical-system" number="yes">
x^3 = y \quad \text{and} \quad y^3 = x.
</md>
</p>
<p>
To solve these equations, we substitute the second equation into the
first one:
<md>
<mrow>
x^3 = (y^3)^3 = y^9 = y \amp\Rightarrow y(y^8 - 1) = 0
\Rightarrow y(y^4 - 1)(y^4 + 1) = 0
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\Rightarrow y(y^2 - 1)(y^2 + 1)(y^4 + 1) = 0
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\Rightarrow y(y - 1)(y + 1)(y^2 + 1)(y^4 + 1) = 0
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\Rightarrow y = -1, 0, 1.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Therefore, the two partials are zero at the points <m>(-1,-1)</m>,
<m>(1,1)</m>, and <m>(0,0)</m>, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-quartic-critical-points"/>. Next we compute the Hessian
<xref ref="eq-hessian"/> and apply the second derivative test. We have
<m>f_{xx} = 12x^2</m>, <m>f_{yy} = 12y^2</m>, and
<m>f_{xy} = f_{yx} = -4</m>, so that
<md xml:id="eq-quartic-hessian" number="yes">
H(x,y) =
\begin{vmatrix}
f_{xx} \amp f_{xy} \\
f_{xy} \amp f_{yy}
\end{vmatrix}
= f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = 144x^2y^2 - 16.
</md>
</p>
<p>
We now evaluate the Hessian at each critical point:
</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>H(0,0) = -16 \lt 0 \Rightarrow</m> the origin <m>(0,0)</m> is a
saddle point.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>H(1,1) = 144 - 16 = 128 \gt 0</m> and
<m>f_{xx}(1,1) = 12 \gt 0 \Rightarrow (1,1)</m> is a local minimum.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>H(-1,-1) = 144 - 16 = 128 \gt 0</m> and
<m>f_{xx}(-1,-1) = 12 \gt 0 \Rightarrow (-1,-1)</m> is a local
minimum.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>
Check out the surface in <xref ref="fig-quartic-video"/>.
</p>

</paragraphs>

<figure xml:id="fig-quartic-video">
<caption>
The surface <m>z = x^4 + y^4 - 4xy</m> has a saddle point at
<m>(0,0,0)</m> and two local minima, at <m>(1,1,-2)</m> and
<m>(-1,-1,-2)</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-quartic-extrema"
       source="videos/quartic_extrema_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/quartic_extrema_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-quartic-critical-points">
<caption>
The critical points of <m>f(x,y) = x^4 + y^4 - 4xy</m> are the
intersections of the curves <m>y = x^3</m> and <m>x = y^3</m>: a saddle
point at <m>(0,0)</m> and local minima at <m>(1,1)</m> and
<m>(-1,-1)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>Two cubic curves on a grid crossing at three points.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A coordinate grid showing the two curves y equals x cubed, in blue, and x equals y cubed, in red. The curves intersect at three points: the origin, which is a saddle point, and the points 1, 1 and negative 1, negative 1, which are local minima.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-ev-quartic-critical-points"><![CDATA[
// The curves y = x^3 (where f_x = 0) and x = y^3 (where f_y = 0) for
// f(x,y) = x^4 + y^4 - 4xy: they meet at the three critical points.
import graph;
unitsize(2cm);

// a light grid with the units marked
pen gridpen = gray(0.82) + linewidth(0.4pt);
for (int k = -1; k <= 1; ++k) {
  if (k != 0) {
    draw((k, -1.85)--(k, 1.85), gridpen);
    draw((-1.85, k)--(1.85, k), gridpen);
    label("$" + string(k) + "$", (k, 0), S, fontsize(9pt));
    label("$" + string(k) + "$", (0, k), W, fontsize(9pt));
  }
}
pen axispen = black + linewidth(0.8pt);
draw((-1.9, 0)--(1.9, 0), axispen, Arrow(6));
draw((0, -1.9)--(0, 1.9), axispen, Arrow(6));
label("$x$", (1.9, 0), E);
label("$y$", (0, 1.9), N);

draw(graph(new pair(real t) { return (t, t^3); }, -1.23, 1.23), blue + linewidth(1.6pt));
draw(graph(new pair(real t) { return (t^3, t); }, -1.23, 1.23), red + linewidth(1.6pt));
label("$y=x^3$", (1.2, 1.2^3), E, blue);
label("$x=y^3$", (1.2^3, 1.2), N, red);

pen saddle = rgb(0.5, 0, 0), minimum = rgb(0, 0.5, 0);
dot((0, 0), saddle + linewidth(5.5pt));
label("$(0,0)$", (0, 0), 2*SE, saddle);
dot((1, 1), minimum + linewidth(5.5pt));
label("$(1,1)$", (1, 1), 2*NW, minimum);
dot((-1, -1), minimum + linewidth(5.5pt));
label("$(-1,-1)$", (-1, -1), 2*SE, minimum);
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-shortest-distance">
<title>Shortest Distance to a Plane</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the shortest distance from the point <m>(1,0,-2)</m> to the plane
<m>x + 2y + z = 4</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<paragraphs xml:id="solution-shortest-distance">
<title>Solution</title>

<p>
We begin by writing the distance between the point <m>(1,0,-2)</m> and
points <m>(x,y,z)</m> on the plane:
<md xml:id="eq-distance-formula" number="yes">
d = \sqrt{(x-1)^2 + y^2 + (z+2)^2}.
</md>
</p>
<p>
Then we use the fact that the points <m>(x,y,z)</m> are on the plane,
<m>z = 4 - x - 2y</m>, to express the distance as a function of
<m>x</m> and <m>y</m> only:
<md xml:id="eq-distance-xy" number="yes">
d = \sqrt{(x-1)^2 + y^2 + (6 - x - 2y)^2}.
</md>
</p>
<p>
We make the observation that to find the shortest distance we can
minimize <m>d^2</m> rather than <m>d</m>:
<md xml:id="eq-distance-squared" number="yes">
d^2 = f(x,y) = (x-1)^2 + y^2 + (6 - x - 2y)^2.
</md>
</p>
<p>
We calculate the partials and set them equal to zero:
<md>
<mrow>
f_x \amp= 2(x-1) - 2(6 - x - 2y) = 4x + 4y - 14 = 0
</mrow>
<mrow>
f_y \amp= 2y - 4(6 - x - 2y) = 4x + 10y - 24 = 0.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
We subtract the first equation from the second one:
<md xml:id="eq-distance-solve" number="yes">
6y = 10 \Rightarrow y = \frac{5}{3} \Rightarrow x = \frac{11}{6}
\Rightarrow \text{The only critical point is } \left(\frac{11}{6}, \frac{5}{3}\right).
</md>
</p>
<p>
We compute the Hessian <xref ref="eq-hessian"/>:
<md xml:id="eq-distance-hessian" number="yes">
H(x,y) =
\begin{vmatrix}
f_{xx} \amp f_{xy} \\
f_{xy} \amp f_{yy}
\end{vmatrix}
= f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = (4)(10) - (4)^2 = 24 \gt 0.
</md>
</p>
<p>
Since <m>H \gt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx} = 4 \gt 0</m>, the point
<m>\left(\frac{11}{6}, \frac{5}{3}\right)</m> is a local minimum.
However, we know that there is a point on the plane that has the
shortest distance to <m>(1,0,-2)</m>, which means
<m>\left(\frac{11}{6}, \frac{5}{3}\right)</m> is in fact where the
absolute minimum of the distance occurs. To find the shortest distance
we plug the point <m>\left(\frac{11}{6}, \frac{5}{3}\right)</m> into
the distance formula, which gives
<md xml:id="eq-shortest-distance" number="yes">
d = \sqrt{\left(\frac{5}{6}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{5}{3}\right)^2
+ \left(\frac{5}{6}\right)^2} = \frac{5}{6}\sqrt{6}.
</md>
</p>
</paragraphs>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-absolute-extrema">
<title>Absolute Maximum and Absolute Minimum</title>

<p>
In the next example, we will see how to find absolute extrema of a
function <m>f(x,y)</m> over a closed bounded region <m>R</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-absolute-extrema">
<title>Absolute Extrema over a Rectangle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of the function
<m>f(x,y) = x^2 - 2xy + 2y</m> on the rectangle
<md xml:id="eq-rectangle-region" number="yes">
R = \{(x,y) : 0 \le x \le 3,\; 0 \le y \le 2\}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<paragraphs xml:id="solution-absolute-extrema">
<title>Solution</title>

<p>
<em>Step (I):</em> We begin by finding the critical points of
<m>f(x,y)</m> that are also in the set <m>R</m>:
<md>
<mrow>
f_x \amp= 2x - 2y = 0 \Rightarrow x = y
</mrow>
<mrow>
f_y \amp= -2x + 2 = 0 \Rightarrow x = 1.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Therefore, the only critical point is <m>(1,1)</m>, with
<m>f(1,1) = 1</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Step (II):</em> We find the extreme values of <m>f(x,y)</m> on the
boundary of <m>R</m>, i.e. along the four edges of the rectangle
denoted by <m>L_1</m>, <m>L_2</m>, <m>L_3</m>, and <m>L_4</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-rectangle-region"/>.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Along the first edge <m>L_1</m>, we have <m>f(x,0) = x^2</m> and since
<m>0 \le x \le 3</m>, the minimum value is <m>f(0,0) = 0</m> and the
maximum value is <m>f(3,0) = 9</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Along the second edge <m>L_2</m>, we have <m>f(3,y) = 9 - 4y</m> and
since <m>0 \le y \le 2</m>, the minimum value is <m>f(3,2) = 1</m> and
the maximum value is <m>f(3,0) = 9</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Along the third edge <m>L_3</m>, we have
<m>f(x,2) = x^2 - 4x + 4 = (x-2)^2</m> and since <m>0 \le x \le 3</m>,
the minimum value is <m>f(2,2) = 0</m> and the maximum value is
<m>f(0,2) = 4</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Along the fourth edge <m>L_4</m>, we have <m>f(0,y) = 2y</m> and since
<m>0 \le y \le 2</m>, the minimum value is <m>f(0,0) = 0</m> and the
maximum value is <m>f(0,2) = 4</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>
<em>Step (III):</em> We compare the values obtained in the previous
steps. The absolute maximum value of <m>f(x,y)</m> on the rectangle
<m>R</m> is <m>f(3,0) = 9</m> and its absolute minimum is
<m>f(0,0) = f(2,2) = 0</m>. The candidate points and the surface are
shown in <xref ref="fig-absolute-extrema-surface-video"/> and
<xref ref="fig-absolute-candidates"/>.
</p>

</paragraphs>

<figure xml:id="fig-rectangle-region-video">
<caption>
The closed bounded rectangle
<m>R = \{(x,y) : 0 \le x \le 3,\; 0 \le y \le 2\}</m> with its four
edges <m>L_1</m>, <m>L_2</m>, <m>L_3</m>, and <m>L_4</m>, and the
critical point <m>(1,1)</m> in its interior.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-rectangle-region"
       source="videos/rectangle_region_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/rectangle_region_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-rectangle-region">
<caption>
The rectangle <m>R</m> and its four edges. To find the absolute
extrema, we evaluate <m>f</m> at the critical points inside <m>R</m>
and compare with the extreme values of <m>f</m> along each edge.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>A rectangle with its four edges labelled and one point marked inside it.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
The rectangle R in the x y plane, with corners at the origin, 3, 0, 3, 2, and 0, 2, shaded gray. Its four edges are labeled L 1 along the bottom, L 2 along the right, L 3 along the top, and L 4 along the left. The critical point 1, 1 is marked inside the rectangle.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-ev-rectangle-region"><![CDATA[
// The rectangle R = [0,3] x [0,2] with its four edges L1, L2, L3, L4 and the critical
// point (1,1) in its interior.
import graph;
unitsize(1.9cm);

// axes through the origin, with arrowheads and no tick marks
void axes(real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax, string xl, string yl) {
  pen axispen = black + linewidth(0.8pt);
  draw((xmin, 0)--(xmax, 0), axispen, Arrow(6));
  draw((0, ymin)--(0, ymax), axispen, Arrow(6));
  if (xl != "") label(xl, (xmax, 0), E);
  if (yl != "") label(yl, (0, ymax), N);
}

fill(box((0, 0), (3, 2)), gray(0.87));
axes(-0.6, 3.9, -0.6, 2.8, "$x$", "$y$");

pen c1 = blue, c2 = red, c3 = rgb(0, 0.5, 0), c4 = rgb(0.9, 0.5, 0);
draw((0, 0)--(3, 0), c1 + linewidth(2.2pt));
draw((3, 0)--(3, 2), c2 + linewidth(2.2pt));
draw((0, 2)--(3, 2), c3 + linewidth(2.2pt));
draw((0, 0)--(0, 2), c4 + linewidth(2.2pt));
label("$L_1$", (1.5, 0), S, c1);
label("$L_2$", (3, 1), E, c2);
label("$L_3$", (1.5, 2), N, c3);
label("$L_4$", (0, 1), W, c4);
label("$3$", (3, 0), S);
label("$2$", (0, 2), W);

label("$R$", (2.2, 1.3));
dot((1, 1), rgb(0.5, 0, 0) + linewidth(5.5pt));
label("$(1,1)$", (1, 1), NE, rgb(0.5, 0, 0));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-absolute-extrema-surface-video">
<caption>
The surface <m>z = x^2 - 2xy + 2y</m> over the rectangle <m>R</m>. The
absolute maximum <m>f(3,0) = 9</m> and the absolute minimum
<m>f(0,0) = f(2,2) = 0</m> occur on the boundary of <m>R</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-absolute-extrema-surface"
       source="videos/absolute_extrema_surface_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/absolute_extrema_surface_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-absolute-candidates">
<caption>
The candidate points for the absolute extrema of
<m>f(x,y) = x^2 - 2xy + 2y</m> on <m>R</m>: the interior critical
point and the extreme points found along the four edges. Comparing the
values of <m>f</m>, the absolute maximum is <m>f(3,0) = 9</m> and the
absolute minimum is <m>f(0,0) = f(2,2) = 0</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>A rectangle with six marked points, each labelled with a value of f.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
The rectangle R with the candidate points for the absolute extrema of f marked and labeled with their function values. The absolute maximum, 9, occurs at 3, 0 and is marked in red. The absolute minimum, 0, occurs at the origin and at 2, 2, both marked in green.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-ev-absolute-candidates"><![CDATA[
// The candidate points for the absolute extrema of f(x,y) = x^2 - 2xy + 2y on R, each
// with its value of f: the maximum 9 at (3,0), the minimum 0 at (0,0) and (2,2).
import graph;
unitsize(1.9cm);

// axes through the origin, with arrowheads and no tick marks
void axes(real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax, string xl, string yl) {
  pen axispen = black + linewidth(0.8pt);
  draw((xmin, 0)--(xmax, 0), axispen, Arrow(6));
  draw((0, ymin)--(0, ymax), axispen, Arrow(6));
  if (xl != "") label(xl, (xmax, 0), E);
  if (yl != "") label(yl, (0, ymax), N);
}

filldraw(box((0, 0), (3, 2)), gray(0.87), black + linewidth(0.8pt));
axes(-0.8, 4.1, -0.7, 2.9, "$x$", "$y$");

pen lo = rgb(0, 0.5, 0), hi = red, crit = rgb(0.5, 0, 0);
void candidate(pair p, string value, pair where, pen c) {
  dot(p, c + linewidth(5.5pt));
  label("$f=" + value + "$", p, where, c);
}
candidate((0, 0), "0", 2*SW, lo);
candidate((2, 2), "0", 2*N, lo);
candidate((3, 0), "9", 2*SE, hi);
candidate((0, 2), "4", 2*NW, black);
candidate((3, 2), "1", 2*NE, black);
candidate((1, 1), "1", 2*NE, crit);
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-equilibrium-stability">
<title>A Physical Application: Equilibrium and Stability</title>

<p>
Consider a marble that rolls on the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> under the
influence of gravity. If we measure heights from the <m>xy</m>-plane,
the potential energy of the marble at the point <m>(x,y)</m> of the
surface is
<md xml:id="eq-potential-energy" number="yes">
U(x,y) = mgz = mg\,f(x,y),
</md>
where <m>m</m> is the mass of the marble and <m>g</m> is the
acceleration due to gravity. The marble is in <term>equilibrium</term>
at a point where the force along the surface vanishes, which happens
exactly where both partial derivatives of the potential energy are
zero:
<md xml:id="eq-equilibrium-condition" number="yes">
\begin{aligned}
U_x \amp = mg\,f_x = 0,\\
U_y \amp = mg\,f_y = 0.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
<p>
In other words, the equilibrium points of the marble are precisely the
critical points of <m>f</m>. The second derivative test then tells us
whether the equilibrium is <term>stable</term> (a displaced marble
rolls back) or <term>unstable</term> (a displaced marble rolls away).
</p>

<example xml:id="example-marble-stability">
<title>Equilibrium and Stability of a Marble</title>
<statement>
<p>
A marble rests at the origin on each of the following three surfaces:
<md xml:id="eq-three-surfaces" number="yes">
\begin{aligned}
\text{(i)} \amp \; z = x^2 + y^2,\\
\text{(ii)} \amp \; z = -x^2 - y^2,\\
\text{(iii)} \amp \; z = \frac{y^2}{3} - \frac{x^2}{3}.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
<p>
Show that <m>(0,0)</m> is an equilibrium point in each case, and
determine whether the equilibrium is stable or unstable.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<paragraphs xml:id="solution-marble-stability">
<title>Solution</title>

<p>
In each case the potential energy is <m>U(x,y) = mg\,f(x,y)</m>, so by
<xref ref="eq-equilibrium-condition"/> the equilibrium points are the
critical points of <m>f</m>. For all three surfaces we have
<m>f_x(0,0) = f_y(0,0) = 0</m>, so the origin is an equilibrium point
in each case. We classify each equilibrium with the second derivative
test, as shown in <xref ref="fig-stability-video"/>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Case (i):</em> For <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2</m> we have
<m>f_{xx} = f_{yy} = 2</m> and <m>f_{xy} = 0</m>, so
<m>H(0,0) = 4 \gt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx} \gt 0</m>, and the origin is a
local minimum of the potential energy. A marble displaced slightly in
any direction rolls back toward the bottom: the equilibrium is
<em>stable</em>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Case (ii):</em> For <m>f(x,y) = -x^2 - y^2</m> we have
<m>f_{xx} = f_{yy} = -2</m> and <m>f_{xy} = 0</m>, so
<m>H(0,0) = 4 \gt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx} \lt 0</m>, and the origin is a
local maximum of the potential energy. A marble displaced slightly in
any direction rolls away from the top: the equilibrium is
<em>unstable</em>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Case (iii):</em> For <m>f(x,y) = y^2/3 - x^2/3</m> we have
<m>f_{xx} = -2/3</m>, <m>f_{yy} = 2/3</m>, and <m>f_{xy} = 0</m>, so
<m>H(0,0) = -4/9 \lt 0</m>, and the origin is a saddle point. Along the
<m>y</m>-axis the potential energy rises, so a marble displaced in the
<m>y</m>-direction rolls back; but along the <m>x</m>-axis the
potential energy falls, so a marble displaced in the
<m>x</m>-direction rolls away. Since some displacements grow, the
equilibrium is <em>unstable</em>.
</p>

<p>
The general principle illustrated by this example, shown in
<xref ref="fig-stability-cross-sections"/>, is that an equilibrium is
stable exactly when the potential energy has a local minimum there.
</p>

</paragraphs>

<figure xml:id="fig-stability-video">
<caption>
A marble at an equilibrium point on each of the three surfaces. On the
bowl <m>z = x^2 + y^2</m> a displaced marble rolls back (stable); on
the dome <m>z = -x^2 - y^2</m> it rolls away (unstable); on the saddle
<m>z = y^2/3 - x^2/3</m> it rolls back along the <m>y</m>-direction but
away along the <m>x</m>-direction (unstable).
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-marble-stability"
       source="videos/stability_marble_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/stability_marble_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-stability-cross-sections">
<caption>
Cross-sections of the potential energy near an equilibrium point. Along
a direction where <m>U</m> has a minimum (blue), the force is
restoring and pushes the marble back: stable. Along a direction where
<m>U</m> has a maximum (red), the force pushes the marble away:
unstable. For the bowl, every cross-section is the blue type; for the
dome, every cross-section is the red type; a saddle has one of each.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>Two parabolas through the origin with force arrows: toward the origin on one, away from it on the other.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
Two cross-sections of the potential energy through an equilibrium point at the origin, where a green marble sits. The blue curve opens upward: the force arrows point back toward the equilibrium, so this direction is stable. The red curve opens downward: the force arrows point away from the equilibrium, so this direction is unstable.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-ev-stability-cross-sections"><![CDATA[
// Two cross-sections of the potential energy U through an equilibrium point: one with
// a minimum (the force pushes the marble back) and one with a maximum (it pushes it away).
import graph;
unitsize(2.3cm);

// axes through the origin, with arrowheads and no tick marks
void axes(real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax, string xl, string yl) {
  pen axispen = black + linewidth(0.8pt);
  draw((xmin, 0)--(xmax, 0), axispen, Arrow(6));
  draw((0, ymin)--(0, ymax), axispen, Arrow(6));
  if (xl != "") label(xl, (xmax, 0), E);
  if (yl != "") label(yl, (0, ymax), N);
}

axes(-1.7, 1.7, -1.85, 1.85, "", "$U$");

real stable(real x) { return 0.9*x^2; }
real unstable(real x) { return -0.9*x^2; }
draw(graph(stable, -1.35, 1.35), blue + linewidth(1.6pt));
draw(graph(unstable, -1.35, 1.35), red + linewidth(1.6pt));
label("stable", (1.2, stable(1.2)), E, blue);
label("unstable", (1.2, unstable(1.2)), E, red);

// the force on a displaced marble: back towards the equilibrium, or away from it
draw((0.95, 0.35)--(0.5, 0.35), blue + linewidth(1.8pt), Arrow(7));
draw((-0.95, 0.35)--(-0.5, 0.35), blue + linewidth(1.8pt), Arrow(7));
draw((0.45, -0.35)--(0.9, -0.35), red + linewidth(1.8pt), Arrow(7));
draw((-0.45, -0.35)--(-0.9, -0.35), red + linewidth(1.8pt), Arrow(7));

dot((0, 0), rgb(0, 0.5, 0) + linewidth(6pt));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

